= Hold a Rally

The Federation is a coalition, and every hall we fill sends a message about which part of it we are. What will we talk about?

- @rally_debt: Debt, deflation and the railroad trusts.
- @rally_hours: The eight-hour day and the right to organize.
- @rally_public: Public ownership of the railroads, mines and banks.
- @rally_clean: Clean government: break the machines and the trusts.
- @rally_equal: One union, one vote, for every worker.

@rally_debt
on-arrival: farmers_fl += 6; small_town_fl += 1; populists_strength += 2; socialists_strength -= 1; unionists_strength -= 1; progressives_dissent += 1

Under a gold standard that makes every dollar of debt heavier each year, the farmers of the plains need no convincing. They fill the grange halls, and the grain elevator co-operatives sign up new members that night.

@rally_hours
on-arrival: industrial_fl += 5; tradesmen_fl += 3; unionists_strength += 2; populists_strength -= 1; progressives_strength -= 1

The craft unions and the mine locals turn out in force. Their message is simple: shorter hours, higher wages, and a law that protects a union card.

@rally_public
on-arrival: industrial_fl += 3; urban_ethnic_fl += 3; farmers_fl += 1; small_town_fl -= 2; business_fl -= 1; socialists_strength += 2; unionists_dissent += 2; progressives_dissent += 3

The old Debsian speeches still move a crowd. The socialists are thrilled, but the small-town press has a field day with the word "nationalization", and the Progressives are visibly uneasy.

@rally_clean
on-arrival: small_town_fl += 4; business_fl += 1; urban_ethnic_fl -= 1; progressives_strength += 2; socialists_dissent += 2

The teachers, clerks and ministers of the small towns like a party that promises to run the machines out of city hall. The urban ethnic wards, whose jobs and favors come from those very machines, are less sure.

@rally_equal
on-arrival: urban_ethnic_fl += 4; industrial_fl += 2; tradesmen_fl -= 5; socialists_strength += 2; progressives_strength += 1; unionists_dissent += 3

A. Philip Randolph takes the platform and asks the hall a plain question: how can a union call itself a union while it shuts a man out over the color of his skin? The industrial locals and the northern Black congregations cheer. Some of the craft unions' hardest members walk out.
